# Bootstrap from zero

Bring up the full stack on a fresh server, top to bottom. Every step has a
verify command — do not continue past a failing verify. This runbook covers
exactly the residue CI cannot test: the k3s install, systemd, hostPort on a
real NIC, real DNS and Let's Encrypt.

Total time: ~30–45 minutes, most of it waiting for ZITADEL's first start.

## 0. Prerequisites

- A server (Ubuntu, {{ target_arch }}) reachable at `{{ server_ipv4 }}`,
  root access, ports 80/443 free (no other web server running).
- DNS: `*.{{ domain }}` (wildcard A record) → `{{ server_ipv4 }}`.
- This repo pushed to `https://github.com/{{ github_owner }}/{{ project_name }}`.
- Locally (or on the agent's machine): `kubectl`, `kubeseal`, `tofu`, `git`
  (the `argocd` CLI is handy but optional — everything works via `kubectl`).

**Verify DNS before anything else:**

```bash
dig +short id.{{ domain }} anything.{{ domain }}
# both → {{ server_ipv4 }}
```

## 1. Install k3s

On the server, as root:

```bash
curl -sfL https://get.k3s.io | INSTALL_K3S_EXEC="--disable=traefik --disable=servicelb" sh -
```

The bundled Traefik and servicelb MUST stay disabled — `cluster/traefik/`
deploys our own Traefik with hostPort 80/443.

Kubeconfig for your user:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/.kube
sudo cp /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml ~/.kube/config
sudo chown "$USER:$USER" ~/.kube/config && chmod 600 ~/.kube/config
export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config
```

**Verify:**

```bash
kubectl get node   # one node, STATUS Ready
```

To operate remotely, add a `tls-san` for your API domain in
`/etc/rancher/k3s/config.yaml` and open 6443 (see §8); for a first
bootstrap, running directly on the server is fine.

## 2. Install Argo CD

```bash
kubectl create namespace argocd
kubectl apply -n argocd \
  -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-cd/v2.13.3/manifests/install.yaml

# Two argocd-cm tweaks this single-node, no-LoadBalancer stack needs (see
# runbooks/incidents/argocd-resource-tracking.md):
#  1. annotation-based resource tracking — the ZITADEL chart provisions runtime
#     secrets labelled app.kubernetes.io/instance=zitadel; Argo's DEFAULT label
#     tracking mistakes them for orphans and prunes them (login-client →
#     zitadel-login never starts).
#  2. custom Ingress health — Traefik runs ClusterIP+hostPort with no
#     LoadBalancer, so Ingresses never get .status.loadBalancer; Argo's default
#     health check would otherwise hang every Ingress at Progressing forever.
kubectl -n argocd patch configmap argocd-cm --type merge -p '{"data":{
  "application.resourceTrackingMethod":"annotation",
  "resource.customizations.health.networking.k8s.io_Ingress":"hs = {} hs.status = \"Healthy\" hs.message = \"single-node hostPort Traefik publishes no load-balancer status\" return hs"
}}'
kubectl -n argocd rollout restart statefulset/argocd-application-controller
```

**Verify:**

```bash
kubectl -n argocd rollout status deploy/argocd-repo-server --timeout=300s
kubectl -n argocd get pods   # server, repo-server, application/applicationset controllers Running
```

## 3. Bootstrap Argo CD from this repo

If the repo is private, first register the **GitHub App** repo credential for
the org that owns it — NEVER a PAT (invariant 4), created directly in the
cluster, never committed (invariant 3). Full recipe in
`runbooks/argocd-github-app.md`; in short, for `{{ github_owner }}`:

```bash
INST=$(gh api /orgs/{{ github_owner }}/installations \
  --jq ".installations[]|select(.app_id==<app-id>).id")
kubectl create secret generic {{ github_owner }}-argocd-secret -n argocd \
  --from-literal=type=git \
  --from-literal=url=https://github.com/{{ github_owner }} \
  --from-literal=githubAppID=<app-id> \
  --from-literal=githubAppInstallationID="$INST" \
  --from-file=githubAppPrivateKey=<app-key.pem>
kubectl label secret {{ github_owner }}-argocd-secret -n argocd \
  argocd.argoproj.io/secret-type=repo-creds
```

(A PUBLIC repo needs none of this.) Then — the only manual `kubectl apply` in
the lifetime of this cluster — apply the root "app of apps":

```bash
kubectl apply -k argocd/
```

Argo CD now reconciles the whole substrate from git (Traefik, cert-manager,
sealed-secrets, rbac, policy, then ZITADEL and apps), ordered by sync waves.

**Verify (cert-manager CRDs take a minute):**

```bash
kubectl -n argocd get applications
# root:               Synced/Healthy
# traefik:            Synced/Healthy
# cluster:            Synced/Healthy   (cert-manager, sealed-secrets, rbac, policy)
# zitadel-base:       OutOfSync        ← expected! sealed secrets missing, next step
# zitadel-postgresql: Progressing → Healthy
# zitadel:            Progressing      ← waits on the sealed secrets too
# apps:               Synced/Healthy   (empty)
kubectl -n kube-system get ds traefik    # DESIRED 1, READY 1
curl -sI http://{{ domain }}             # answers (404 is fine — Traefik is up)
```

## 4. Seal the ZITADEL bootstrap secrets

```bash
./scripts/fetch-sealing-cert.sh
./scripts/seal-zitadel-secrets.sh
git add sealed-secrets-public-key.pem zitadel/sealed-zitadel-*.yaml
git commit -m "Seal ZITADEL bootstrap secrets" && git push
# Argo CD picks this up on its next poll (~3 min); to apply immediately:
kubectl -n argocd patch app zitadel-base --type merge \
  -p '{"operation":{"sync":{}}}'   # or: argocd app sync zitadel-base
```

**Verify (first start takes 5–15 min — init and setup jobs run first):**

```bash
kubectl -n argocd get app zitadel-base zitadel-postgresql zitadel  # → Synced/Healthy
kubectl -n zitadel get pods             # zitadel + zitadel-login Running
curl -fsS https://id.{{ domain }}/.well-known/openid-configuration | head -3
```

If the ZITADEL sync hangs in install >30s: pull the init pod logs IMMEDIATELY —
`runbooks/incidents/zitadel-bootstrap.md` (lesson learned section). Do not wait.

**Verify TLS is real:**

```bash
kubectl get certificates -A             # all Ready=True
curl -fsSI https://id.{{ domain }} > /dev/null && echo TLS OK
```

## 5. ZITADEL bootstrap (instance/org only — NOT identity content)

This stands up the org and the first admin user. **Identity content** —
projects, roles, OIDC clients and above all who is in which role — is **not**
done here: it is API-managed at runtime (invariant 6,
`runbooks/zitadel-identity-via-api.md`). Do not commit memberships to tofu.

Mint an operator service-user PAT/key in the ZITADEL console (Instance →
Service Users → IAM-owner), provide it **per session** (never commit it, never
store it in the cluster as a standing credential), then:

```bash
kubectl create namespace terraform-state
cd tofu/zitadel && tofu init && tofu apply   # provider reads the per-session operator credential
```

**Verify:**

```bash
tofu output -raw admin_initial_password    # read once, store in password manager
```

Log in at `https://id.{{ domain }}` as `admin` with that password (forced
change on first login), then do all further identity work via the console /
API — `runbooks/zitadel-identity-via-api.md`.

## 6. ForwardAuth front door (optional but recommended)

Follow `patterns/app-forwardauth/README.md` — seal the oauth2-proxy
credentials from the tofu outputs, register the pattern, push.

**Verify:**

```bash
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{redirect_url}\n' \
  "https://auth.{{ domain }}/oauth2/start?rd=https://{{ domain }}/"
# → https://id.{{ domain }}/...
```

## 7. Private container images — the registry pull credential

Apps this community builds itself ship as **private** GHCR packages, and a
private package does not pull itself: every namespace that runs such an image
needs a registry credential. Do this section before the first own app goes
live; skip it only while every image in the cluster is public.

> **Never "fix" a failing pull by making the package public.** A container
> image is not just code. A static-site build (Astro, Hugo, Eleventy) compiles
> `src/content/` **into the image**, so a public package hands out exactly the
> member data the login was there to protect — no cluster involved, no log
> entry, `docker pull` is enough. Details and the visibility check:
> `patterns/app-deploy-ci/README.md`, "The package stays private".

**The decision:** one **classic PAT whose only scope is `read:packages`**,
installed as the `imagePullSecret` `ghcr-pull` in each app namespace. Not a
GitHub App, not a fine-grained PAT — both are dead ends on GHCR; read "Why not
the GitHub App" and "Why not a fine-grained PAT" at the end of this section
before you try to improve this.

A pull credential is an **infrastructure credential**: created directly with
`kubectl`, **never committed, not even sealed** (`AGENTS.md` inv. 3). Invariant
4's "never a PAT" governs **Argo CD's git authentication** — git auth and
registry auth are different problems with different answers, and this is the
answer for registry auth.

### 7.1 Mint the token

GitHub → Settings → Developer settings → **Personal access tokens (classic)** →
Generate new token:

- **Scope: `read:packages`, and nothing else.** Not `write:packages`, not
  `repo`. The cluster only ever pulls; CI pushes with its own `GITHUB_TOKEN`
  (`patterns/app-deploy-ci/`).
- **Expiration: a real date, at most one year.** "No expiration" is forbidden
  here — an immortal credential is one nobody ever reviews.
- The token belongs to a **person**. Write down which account and which expiry
  date; §7.3 records both in the cluster and you need them at renewal.

Keep the token itself in the operator's password manager, next to the Argo CD
GitHub App key (`runbooks/argocd-github-app.md`).

### 7.2 Prove the token before it goes near the cluster

A wrong registry credential does not fail loudly — `docker login ghcr.io`
succeeds with almost any input and only the pull says `denied`. Ask the
registry directly instead (needs `jq` on the machine you run this from):

```bash
read -rs GHCR_PAT           # paste the token: no echo, no shell history
GH_USER=<github-login-that-owns-the-token>
PKG={{ github_owner }}/<app>          # owner/package, lowercase

TOKEN=$(curl -sS -u "$GH_USER:$GHCR_PAT" \
  "https://ghcr.io/token?service=ghcr.io&scope=repository:$PKG:pull" \
  | jq -r '.token // empty')
[ -n "$TOKEN" ] || echo "FAIL: ghcr.io rejected the credentials outright"
curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" "https://ghcr.io/v2/$PKG/tags/list"
```

- `200` — the token may pull this package. Continue.
- `401` — token wrong, expired, or missing `read:packages`.
- `403` — token valid, but this account has no access to that package.

Do not continue on anything but `200`. A bad credential in the cluster surfaces
much later, as `ImagePullBackOff` in the middle of a rollout.

### 7.3 Install it in the app namespace

```bash
APP=<app>          # the namespace scripts/new-app.sh created

kubectl create secret docker-registry ghcr-pull -n "$APP" \
  --docker-server=ghcr.io \
  --docker-username="$GH_USER" \
  --docker-password="$GHCR_PAT"

# Mandatory: the credential must say who owns it and when it dies.
kubectl annotate secret ghcr-pull -n "$APP" \
  owner="<person or role responsible>" \
  expires="<YYYY-MM-DD from §7.1>"
```

Repeat for every app namespace — one namespace per app (`apps/README.md`), so
one `ghcr-pull` per app; the same token every time, it is one credential
copied. An app with PR previews is the exception — §7.6. When you are done:

```bash
unset GHCR_PAT
```

### 7.4 Reference it from the Deployment

The secret is not used unless the Pod asks for it. In the app repo's
`deploy/overlays/production/deployment.yaml` (`patterns/app-deploy-ci/`):

```yaml
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      imagePullSecrets:
        - name: ghcr-pull
      containers:
        - name: app
          image: ghcr.io/{{ github_owner }}/<app>
```

These lines are safe in git — they name a secret, they do not contain one.

### 7.5 Verify — with a real pull

Do not trust "the secret exists". Pull the image:

```bash
kubectl -n "$APP" run ghcr-pullcheck --restart=Never \
  --image=ghcr.io/{{ github_owner }}/<app>:latest \
  --overrides='{"spec":{"imagePullSecrets":[{"name":"ghcr-pull"}]}}'
sleep 20
kubectl -n "$APP" get pod ghcr-pullcheck \
  -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[0].state.waiting.reason}{"\n"}'
kubectl -n "$APP" delete pod ghcr-pullcheck --now
```

Empty output, `CrashLoopBackOff` or `ContainerCreating` all mean **the image
came down** — the credential works; whether the container then runs is not this
test's business. `ErrImagePull` or `ImagePullBackOff` mean the credential was
rejected: `kubectl -n "$APP" describe pod ghcr-pullcheck` carries the
registry's own message before you delete it.

Credential inventory across the cluster:

```bash
kubectl get secret -A --field-selector type=kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson \
  -o custom-columns=NS:.metadata.namespace,SECRET:.metadata.name,\
OWNER:.metadata.annotations.owner,EXPIRES:.metadata.annotations.expires
# every row must name an owner and a date — `<none>` is a defect, fix it now
```

### 7.6 PR previews — the credential moves to the node

A preview namespace (`<app>-pr-<n>`) is created by Argo CD the moment a PR
opens, so no hand-made secret can be in it in time. For an app that has both
PR previews (`patterns/app-preview-prs/`) and a private image, the same token
goes into the node's containerd config instead, where it covers every
namespace on this node — including ones that do not exist yet:

```yaml
# /etc/rancher/k3s/registries.yaml   (on the server, root-owned, mode 0600)
configs:
  ghcr.io:
    auth:
      username: <github-login>
      password: <the read:packages PAT>
```

```bash
sudo chmod 600 /etc/rancher/k3s/registries.yaml
sudo systemctl restart k3s        # containerd reads this file only at start
```

That covers the app's production namespace too, so such an app needs no
per-namespace `ghcr-pull` — §7.3 stays the rule for apps without previews.
This is a node-wide credential: every workload on the node can then pull every
private package the token can reach. On a single node with one operator that
is an accepted trade-off (inv. 8); it would not be on a shared cluster.

### 7.7 Renewal — the price of this decision

The honest downside of a PAT: it hangs on a personal account and it **expires**.
Nothing in the cluster warns you; pulls simply start failing, and only for Pods
that restart, so the failure looks random. Therefore:

- The `owner` / `expires` annotations from §7.3 are not decoration. Check them
  whenever you touch the cluster (the inventory command in §7.5 is in
  `AGENTS.md`'s verify block for that reason).
- Renew **before** the date: mint a new token (§7.1), re-run §7.2, then
  `kubectl delete secret ghcr-pull -n <app>` and recreate it (§7.3) with a new
  `expires`. Re-verify with §7.5. Revoke the old token afterwards.
- When the owning person leaves, the credential leaves with them. Reissue it on
  another account the same day — this is the one credential in this stack with
  a human single point of failure.
- **Rebuild note:** like every infrastructure credential this is not
  reproducible from git (inv. 3). After a cluster rebuild, redo §7.3 for every
  app namespace from the token in the password manager.

### Why not the GitHub App

The instinctive move is to reuse the Argo CD GitHub App — one credential for
git *and* registry. It does not work, for two independent reasons:

1. **An installation token lives one hour.** An `imagePullSecret` is a static
   `dockerconfigjson` blob; kubelet re-reads it but nothing refreshes it. You
   would need a CronJob that every ~45 minutes signs a JWT with the App's
   private key, exchanges it for an installation token and rewrites the secret
   in every app namespace. That is a moving part on a single-node cluster whose
   failure mode is silent: it dies, nothing changes for an hour, and then the
   next Pod that restarts cannot pull. Nobody connects the two.
2. **GHCR does not accept App installation tokens at all** — `docker login`
   appears to succeed and the pull answers `denied`. GitHub has confirmed this
   as a platform limitation.

So the App would be a scheduled token-refresh machine bolted onto a registry
that rejects its tokens anyway. Do not build it.

### Why not a fine-grained PAT

Fine-grained PATs are the better credential in every other respect — scoped to
one owner, one permission, revocable — so this looks like an obvious upgrade.
**GitHub Packages does not support them.** There is no `Packages` permission in
the fine-grained permission set; GitHub's own docs list "using fine-grained
personal access token to access Packages" under the known feature gaps and
state that "GitHub Packages only supports authentication using a personal access
token (classic)". Tracking issue: `github/roadmap#558`, open since 2022.

The failure mode is the trap: the token *is* accepted by `docker login`, and
only the pull answers `denied`. If you find yourself debugging a mysterious
`ImagePullBackOff`, check the token type first. When the roadmap issue closes,
switching is a one-line change in §7.1 — until then, classic with a single
scope is as small as this credential gets.

## 8. (Optional) CI deploys via GitHub Actions OIDC

With Argo CD, deployments are **pull-based**: production rollouts and PR
previews are reconciled by Argo CD from git (`patterns/app-preview-prs/`), so an
app's CI needs **no** cluster access at all — it only builds and pushes images.
This section is therefore optional, for the rare app that genuinely needs to
`kubectl apply` into its own namespace (it then gets the namespace-scoped
`preview-deployer` role, never cluster-admin).

To let such a CI authenticate without a stored kubeconfig, configure the k3s
API server as an OIDC consumer of GitHub Actions tokens. In
`/etc/rancher/k3s/config.yaml`:

```yaml
kube-apiserver-arg:
  - "oidc-issuer-url=https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com"
  - "oidc-client-id={{ project_name }}"
  - "oidc-username-claim=sub"
  - "oidc-groups-claim=repository"
tls-san:
  - api.{{ domain }}
```

```bash
sudo systemctl restart k3s
sudo ufw allow 6443/tcp comment 'k3s API for GitHub Actions OIDC'
sudo ufw reload
```

Source `Anywhere` is acceptable: GitHub's runner IPs rotate and the API
enforces OIDC auth — unauthenticated requests get 401. If this rule is
missing, CI deploys fail with `dial tcp {{ server_ipv4 }}:6443: i/o timeout`.

CI identities authenticate as
`https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com#repo:<owner>/<repo>:<ref>`. Bind
such a subject to the `preview-deployer` ClusterRole with a namespace-scoped
RoleBinding (the `cluster/rbac/` role exists for exactly this). Never bind them
to cluster-admin (invariant) — the policy in `cluster/policy/` denies it anyway.

**Verify:** a `kubectl --token=<github-oidc-token>` from a workflow can
deploy into its app namespace and nothing else.

## 9. Done — handover to normal operation

```bash
kubectl -n argocd get applications    # everything Synced/Healthy
kubectl get pods -A | grep -vE 'Running|Completed'   # empty
```

From here on: changes happen via git (`AGENTS.md`), apps via
`scripts/new-app.sh`, identities via `tofu/zitadel/`. If anything bites you
during bootstrap that this runbook did not predict: fix the runbook, and if
the lesson generalizes, PR it back to the template.
